A||||10||duke||2.5L oil pump||||1||
Z||000000||duke||10-08-2001||12:49 PM||||Is there any way to check an oil pump without removing it? I have one that seems to be bad but I want to check it before I remove it. Can you remove the shaft that goes from the cam to the pump and insert a rod connected to a drill to spin it? Is this possible? Any other ideas? Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||DaveL||10-08-2001||03:55 PM||||There is no way I know of to really check the function of an oil pump physically. Just have some one test the actual pressure through other means if you don't trust your dash guage.<P>Can't be replaced without removing engine.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||10-08-2001||09:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by duke:<BR><B>Is there any way to check an oil pump without removing it? I have one that seems to be bad but I want to check it before I remove it. Can you remove the shaft that goes from the cam to the pump and insert a rod connected to a drill to spin it? Is this possible? Any other ideas? Thanks.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Duke!<BR>Yes, there is a shaft called a pre-oiler that you can make or buy, that slips on the oil pump shaft after you remove the distributor...<BR>But what are your simptoms?<BR>Low pressure? Check with a mechanical gage, as the senders do go bad...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||duke||10-08-2001||11:56 PM||||I checked my pressure with a mechanical guage and it reads zero. Can I purcahse this pre oiler at a local parts store? Do you just attach it to a drill and spin it? ||||reg||1
Z||000004||88CoupeV6||10-09-2001||11:51 AM||||I would just replace it anyway. I would use I high volume one. cheap insurance.<P>If it is at 0 the engine must be knocking like mad. I hope you only had it running for a sec. <BR>If there is no oil pressure. The damage is done. Hate to say it but better do the rod bearings at the same time. Easy job if you have the pan off for a new pump.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsig.jpg">    <BR>:D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||4
Z||000005||duke||12-02-2001||01:36 PM||||Has anyone actually checked the pressure on a Duke using this pre oiler on a drill? I tried it and there is still just a trickle coming out of the oil sending unit hole. This is even with a new pump. Doesn't the engine have to be running in order to get significant pressure from there?<BR>Looking for ideas before I fire it up again.||||reg||8
Z||000006||Fierowrecker||12-02-2001||02:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by duke:<BR><B>Has anyone actually checked the pressure on a Duke using this pre oiler on a drill? I tried it and there is still just a trickle coming out of the oil sending unit hole. This is even with a new pump. Doesn't the engine have to be running in order to get significant pressure from there?<BR>Looking for ideas before I fire it up again.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Duke!<BR>Check the hole the sender screws into, it may be plugged...<BR>The pre-oiler will build a small amount of pressure, but not as much as a running engine...<BR>Have you changed the filter by chance?<BR>Was the engine knocking when you started it last?<BR>BTW, where you at?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||maryjane||12-02-2001||03:59 PM||||A little more info please. Did the pressure just drop suddenly? Did you do any work on it recently. I used the pre-oiler thing(actually, I made mine) on the end of a larger air diegrinder, before I installed a rebuilt engine. New oil pump/bearings. Spun it pretty fast and oil came up on the valve train pretty good.||||reg||1
Z||000008||duke||12-02-2001||04:24 PM||||The motor was completely rebuilt. New crank, cam, bearings, pump, oil, filter, etc,etc. The block was hot tanked and I blew out all of the oil passages with compressed air before reassembling. I spun the oil pump out of the motor first in a can of oil, and it pumped fine with the pickup tube and screen attached. In the motor however, I only get a little bit.||||reg||1
Z||000009||duke||12-02-2001||04:27 PM||||One question I forgot to ask. Do you spin the pump shaft clockwise or counter-clockwise in the motor?||||reg||1
Z||000010||racefan||12-04-2001||09:40 PM||||clockwise...from what I remember...anyone???||||reg||1
